% ============================================================================
% chamber-exclusive-power — the lawmaking power each chamber of Congress
% holds EXCLUSIVELY (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% The two chambers of Congress are equal in how they function, but each can
% originate one kind of legislation the other cannot. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? chamber_exclusive_power(house_of_representatives, $P)
%       % initiate_tax_and_revenue_legislation
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `chamber_exclusive_power(chamber, power)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the power WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any power not one of these two source-cited rows.
% The query also runs BACKWARD, which is the direction a learner is usually
% quizzed in:
%
%     ? chamber_exclusive_power($C, initiate_tax_and_revenue_legislation)
%       % house_of_representatives
%
% FIFTH library in `civics/`, and the FIRST from a source other than the
% "Branches of the U.S. government" page — that page is now fully decoded
% by `government-branch-member.adj`, `congress-chamber.adj`,
% `checks-and-balances.adj`, and the derived `chamber-branch.adj`, so this
% one opens a new page (and a new axis) rather than restating a mined one.
%
% ATOM REUSE IS DELIBERATE. `house_of_representatives` and `senate` are the
% exact atoms `congress-chamber.adj` already binds, so this table composes
% with the shipped civics graph instead of forking a parallel vocabulary.
% Chained through `chamber-branch.adj`'s derived `chamber_branch`, a
% learner can get from an exclusive power all the way to a branch:
%
%     chamber_exclusive_power(house_of_representatives, initiate_tax_and_revenue_legislation)
%     chamber_branch(house_of_representatives, legislative)
%
% Note the source writes "the House" in both of these sentences while
% naming it "The U.S. House of Representatives" elsewhere on the site; the
% longer atom is used here because it is the one the sibling table already
% established, and a chamber vocabulary that disagrees with itself would
% silently break every composition above.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "How laws are made" page,
% "How the House and Senate's lawmaking procedures are different" section,
% which states each exclusive power as its own bullet:
%
%     chamber (this table's atom)   power (this table's atom)                             quote (verbatim)
%     ---------------------------   ---------------------------------------------------   -----------------------------------------------------
%     house_of_representatives      initiate_tax_and_revenue_legislation                   "Only the House can initiate tax and revenue-related
%                                                                                             legislation"
%     senate                        draft_legislation_on_presidential_nominations_and_     "Only the Senate can draft legislation related to
%                                     treaties                                                presidential nominations and treaties"
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch) — confirmed both sentences
% appear verbatim as the first two bullets of that section's list, each
% beginning "Only the", which is what makes them EXCLUSIVE powers rather
% than merely powers the chamber happens to have. `trust authoritative` —
% USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to
% government information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact
% being recalled, the same tier the four sibling civics libraries cite.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - "While the House processes legislation through a majority vote, the
%     Senate does so through deliberation and debate prior to voting." —
%     a real sentence in the same section, and a genuine House/Senate
%     contrast, but it describes a PROCEDURE each chamber uses, not a kind
%     of legislation only that chamber may originate. It is a different
%     axis and belongs in its own table, not flattened in here beside two
%     "Only the ..." powers.
%   - The section's own framing that the chambers are "equal in how they
%     function" is a characterization of the pair, not a power held by
%     either one.
%   - Any power not one of these two — including every act in
%     `checks-and-balances.adj`, which holds what one part of government
%     can do TO another rather than what one chamber may exclusively
%     ORIGINATE — is deliberately NOT a row. In particular
%     `checks_and_balances(congress, confirm_or_reject, presidential_
%     nominees)` is a distinct fact from this table's senate row: confirming
%     a nominee is a check Congress applies to the executive; drafting
%     legislation RELATED TO nominations and treaties is a lawmaking power
%     the Senate holds alone.
%
% NAMING NOTE, to save a future reader a grep: `money/us-bills.adj` and
% `money/bill-back-vignette.adj` use the atom `bill` for CURRENCY. That is
% a homonym, not a collision — this table names no `bill` atom at all, and
% legislative bills are a separate subject from banknotes.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table chamber_exclusive_power {
    columns chamber, power

    row (house_of_representatives, initiate_tax_and_revenue_legislation)
    row (senate, draft_legislation_on_presidential_nominations_and_treaties)

    source "Only the House can initiate tax and revenue-related legislation"
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Only the Senate can draft legislation related to presidential nominations and treaties" locator "https://www.usa.gov/how-laws-are-made"
}
